Register your own viewer for a format the library doesn't know, or override a built-in by
reusing its `id`. `match` returns a score and the highest non-zero one wins, so you can take
over a type without forking anything.

```js
import { registerViewer } from 'fileon';

const unregister = registerViewer({
  id: 'dicom',
  label: 'DICOM',
  match: (source) => (source.ext === 'dcm' ? 1 : 0),
  load: () => import('./dicom-viewer.js'), // default export is the factory
});
```

`registerViewer` returns a function that removes it again, which is what you want in tests and
in hot-reloaded dev servers.

## The factory

`load()` is a dynamic import so your parser stays out of the entry chunk. Its default export
receives a context and returns an instance.

```js
// dicom-viewer.js
export default async function dicomViewer({ source, container, signal, config, setStatus }) {
  setStatus('Decoding…');

  const buffer = await source.arrayBuffer({ signal, onProgress: () => {} });
  if (signal.aborted) throw new DOMException('aborted', 'AbortError');

  const element = renderDicom(buffer);
  container.append(element);
  setStatus('');

  return {
    element,
    destroy: () => element.remove(),
    actions: [
      { id: 'invert', label: 'Invert', run: () => element.classList.toggle('is-inverted') },
    ],
  };
}
```

### Context

| Field             | Purpose                                                    |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `source`          | The resolved file: `arrayBuffer()`, `text()`, `name`, `ext` |
| `container`       | Append your DOM here                                        |
| `signal`          | Aborts when the user navigates away or closes               |
| `config`          | The live configuration                                      |
| `setStatus`       | One line of text in the toolbar                             |
| `setProgress`     | `(loaded, total)` for the progress bar                      |
| `requestFallback` | Give up and let the next viewer try                         |

### Instance

Everything but `element` is optional. Implement what your format supports and the toolbar
adapts: `destroy`, `actions`, `pageCount`, `goToPage`, `onPageChange`, `zoomIn`, `zoomOut`,
`zoomReset`, `onZoomChange`.

## Overriding a built-in

Reuse the id to replace it outright:

```js
registerViewer({
  id: 'pdf', // same id as the built-in, so it is replaced
  label: 'PDF',
  match: (source) => (source.kind === 'pdf' ? 1 : 0),
  load: () => import('./my-pdf-viewer.js'),
});
```

Or score higher and leave the original registered as a fallback:

```js
registerViewer({
  id: 'pdf-annotated',
  label: 'PDF',
  match: (source) => (source.ext === 'pdf' && source.url.includes('/annotated/') ? 2 : 0),
  load: () => import('./annotated-pdf.js'),
});
```

## Throwing usefully

Throw a `PreviewError` so the panel can offer the right recovery:

```js
import { PreviewError } from 'fileon';

throw new PreviewError('DECODE', 'This scan could not be read.', {
  hint: 'It may be a compressed transfer syntax this build does not support.',
});
```

import { Aside } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';

Every failure arrives as a `PreviewError` carrying a stable `code`. The built-in error panel
picks its recovery buttons from the same code, so what the reader sees and what your handler
receives never disagree.

| Code          | Meaning                                                      |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `CORS`        | The server did not allow this page to read the file           |
| `HTTP`        | Non-2xx response; `error.status` holds the code               |
| `NETWORK`     | The connection failed mid-transfer                            |
| `TOO_LARGE`   | Larger than `maxBytes`                                        |
| `PASSWORD`    | Encrypted PDF, workbook or document                           |
| `DECODE`      | Corrupt, or not the format it claimed to be                   |
| `UNSUPPORTED` | No viewer matched this type                                   |
| `DEPENDENCY`  | A parser chunk could not be loaded                            |
| `EMPTY`       | There was nothing to show                                     |
| `ABORTED`     | Superseded by another load, or closed                         |

```js
openPreview(url, {
  onError: ({ error }) => {
    if (error.code === 'CORS') showCorsHelp();
    else track('preview_failed', { code: error.code, status: error.status });
  },
});
```

## Shape

```ts
class PreviewError extends Error {
  code: PreviewErrorCode;
  /** A second line telling the reader what they can do about it. */
  hint?: string;
  /** Present when code === 'HTTP'. */
  status?: number;
  cause?: unknown;
}
```

<Aside title="Why CORS and NETWORK are separate but ambiguous">
  A browser cannot distinguish a CORS rejection from a dead server — both surface to script as
  an opaque `TypeError: Failed to fetch`. The library reports that case as `CORS` and says so
  plainly in the message rather than guessing which one actually happened.
</Aside>

## Recovery affordances

The error panel chooses buttons from the code, so you usually do not need to build your own:

- `HTTP`, `NETWORK` — retry, and download
- `CORS` — open in a new tab
- `UNSUPPORTED`, `DECODE` — download, and the Office viewer where it applies
- `PASSWORD` — download

All four bubble and cross the shadow boundary, so you can listen on an ancestor rather than on
the element itself.

| Event       | `detail`                         | Fires when                       |
| ----------- | -------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `fp-load`   | `{ source, viewer }`             | A viewer finished rendering      |
| `fp-error`  | `{ source, error }`              | A load or decode failed          |
| `fp-change` | `{ index, source }`              | The gallery moved to another file |
| `fp-close`  | —                                | The modal closed                  |

`source` is the resolved `Source`: `name`, `size`, `kind`, `ext`, `mime` and `url`.
`viewer` is the winning viewer's id, such as `pdf`, `sheet` or `doc`.

```js
document.addEventListener('fp-load', (event) => {
  const { source, viewer } = event.detail;
  analytics.track('preview_opened', { kind: source.kind, viewer });
});
```

The callback options on `openPreview()` are a thin wrapper over the same events, rebound on
each call so a second `openPreview()` never accumulates listeners from the first.

```js
openPreview(url, {
  onLoad: ({ viewer }) => {},
  onError: ({ error }) => {},
});
```
